  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance for afternoon showers and thunderstorms \nfrom Bangor north today. Areas of dense fog could develop again \ntonight, most likely in areas which receive rain from the \nshowers and storms of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nUnsettled weather continues through the day today as a weak \nfront drops through the forecast area this afternoon. Combined \nwith peak heating hours and lingering moisture, isolated to \nscattered showers with a few thunderstorms will be possible, and\nmost likely across the Central Highlands up through the \nnorth... all areas which will could see the FROPA aligning with \npeak heating. As the boundary approaches the coast, the sun will\nbegin to set, and a weak sea breeze could lead to a more stable\nmarine layer Downeast, though additional storms may develop \nalong any interaction between the weak front and the sea breeze \nboundary within the Interior Downeast region through the Bangor \narea early this evening. To support thunderstorms, CAPE seems to\nbe forecast in good amounts, with upwards of 1000 J/kg \navailable from various CAMs. This forecast left the NBM for \nforecast shower coverage, but it can be noted that the RRFS \nincludes a solution with much more isolated shower coverage than\nother CAMs guidances and blends, and if the front is weaker or \nthe area dries out more quickly than forecast, this could be the\nsolution with far less rain.\n\nWherever rain falls, increased surface moisture combined with\ndropping temperatures behind the front could lead to the return\nof areas of dense fog tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be strong with gusty winds and small\nhail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front crosses the area Friday on the leading edge of a \nfull latitude trough pushing into the region, with the added \nsupport of a northern stream shortwave pushing through the \ntrough around that time. With the potential for 500-1500 J/kg of\ninstability and 25-35KT of bulk shear, there is a risk of some \nlocally strong thunderstorms ahead of the front late Thursday \ninto Friday afternoon, with the main threat expected to be \nstrong gusty winds. The greatest chance for storms is likely to \nbe concentrated over northern and central areas while more \nsouthern areas are likely to remain too stable due to southerly \nflow.",
